  "id": "Compound::DB00895 - Gene::2205",
  "verbalization": "Benzylpenicilloyl Polylysine is shown to bind Fcer1g, a subunit of the IgE receptor, through multiple paths with high confidence scores. This binding is linked to the participation of Fcer1g in IgE receptor activity, which in turn is involved in the participation of Fcer1a. The evidence suggests a mechanistic pathway where Benzylpenicilloye Polylysine modulates the IgE receptor complex, potentially influencing immune responses. The overlapping roles of Fcer1g in different biological processes, such as IgE binding and receptor activity, indicate a shared functional context that supports the proposed relationship between the drug and Fcer1a. The integrated evidence implies that the drug may interact with the IgE receptor complex, affecting its function and possibly contributing to immune-related outcomes. The consistent binding to Fcer1g across different paths strengthens the hypothesis, though further validation is needed to confirm the direct effect on Fcer1a.",
